{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789485257,"nonce":"clkw0BBGFMGy4x7wp0EphOkw8UA=","title":"A list read back from a dict or a box is appended to as a copy","message":"```python\none = {\"y\": [1, 2]}\none[\"y\"].append(3)\nprint(one)            # {'y': [1, 2]}, CPython prints {'y': [1, 2, 3]}\nlst = [1, 2]\nthree = {\"z\": lst}\nthree[\"z\"].append(3)\nprint(three, lst)     # neither shows the 3\n```\n\nA list travels as its header struct (pointer, length, capacity); boxing it into a dynamic slot stores a copy of the header, and reading it back copies it again, so an append through the read-back copy grows that copy and leaves the stored header behind. A list is a reference type in Python: every name and every slot that holds it must see the same storage.\n\nThe fix is for a boxed list to carry a pointer to one heap-allocated header that every holder shares (the box's `data` pointing at the header, not at a copy of it), or for lists to be heap headers throughout. Same shape for lists inside lists and instance fields, which need checking.","files":null}]}